# TeamCity / Bitbucket to GitHub Migration Guidelines

Draft v0.1

## Purpose

This document provides high-level guidance for application teams planning migration from TeamCity and Bitbucket to GitHub and GitHub Actions.

At the time of writing, the target state, migration tooling, and organizational standards are still evolving. Therefore, this document should be treated as guidance rather than a strict migration procedure.

Individual applications may require additional activities not covered here.

---

## Assumptions

The following assumptions were used when preparing this document:

- source code currently resides in Bitbucket
- CI pipelines currently run in TeamCity
- artifacts are published to approved corporate repositories
- application teams remain responsible for their own applications
- platform teams provide migration guidance and shared tooling where available

If any of the above assumptions are incorrect for a given application, additional migration work may be required.

---

## What This Document Is Not

This document does not attempt to:

- redesign application architecture
- modernize build processes
- upgrade frameworks
- migrate deployment platforms
- remove technical debt

Migration to GitHub should not automatically be treated as an opportunity to rewrite existing delivery processes.

---

## Recommended Migration Phases

### Phase 0 - Inventory

Before any migration work starts, teams should understand what currently exists.

Suggested inventory items:

- source repositories
- TeamCity projects
- build configurations
- templates
- snapshot dependencies
- artifact dependencies
- deployment dependencies
- secrets and credentials
- agent-specific requirements
- external integrations

In practice, this phase is often underestimated and becomes the source of most migration issues later.

Deliverable:

Basic migration inventory.

### Phase 1 - Repository Migration

Move source code from Bitbucket to GitHub.

Recommended validation:

- commit history preserved
- tags preserved
- branches preserved
- permissions reviewed
- branch protection configured
- CODEOWNERS configured where appropriate

Deliverable:

Working GitHub repository.

### Phase 2 - Build Configuration Review

Review how the application is currently built.

Particular attention should be paid to:

- inline TeamCity scripts
- build parameters
- secure parameters
- shared templates
- meta-runners
- manually maintained agent software

One common risk is discovering that part of the build logic exists only in TeamCity UI and is not stored in source control.

Deliverable:

Documented build process.

### Phase 3 - Configuration Mapping

Determine how existing TeamCity concepts map to GitHub.

| TeamCity | GitHub |
|-----------|---------|
| Build Configuration | Workflow |
| Build Step | Job / Step |
| Parameter | Variable |
| Password Parameter | Secret |
| Agent Pool | Runner Group |
| Template | Reusable Workflow |
| Trigger | Workflow Trigger |

Not every TeamCity feature has a direct GitHub equivalent.

Additional design work may be required for more complex build chains.

Deliverable:

Migration design.

### Phase 4 - GitHub Actions Implementation

Create GitHub workflows.

Where possible, teams should prefer:

- reusable workflows
- approved organization actions
- centralized runner strategy
- approved secrets management approach

Avoid copying workflow implementations between repositories unless there is a clear reason to do so.

Deliverable:

Functional GitHub Actions workflow.

### Phase 5 - Validation

Before cutover, verify:

- build execution
- unit tests
- artifact publication
- deployment execution
- approval flows
- rollback procedures

Running TeamCity and GitHub Actions in parallel for a limited period may reduce migration risk.

Deliverable:

Validated workflow.

### Phase 6 - Cutover

After validation:

- disable TeamCity triggers
- update documentation
- notify stakeholders
- establish support ownership

Deliverable:

GitHub Actions becomes the primary CI platform.

---

## Common Risks

### Hidden TeamCity Logic

Build logic exists only in TeamCity UI.

### Agent Drift

Build depends on software installed manually on TeamCity agents.

### Missing Secrets

Credentials were never documented outside TeamCity.

### Build Chain Complexity

Dependencies between build configurations were not fully understood.

### Migration Scope Expansion

Migration effort becomes mixed with unrelated modernization work.

This is usually one of the largest sources of delay.

---

## Suggested Success Criteria

Migration can be considered complete when:

- source code is hosted in GitHub
- builds execute successfully in GitHub Actions
- artifacts are produced correctly
- deployment processes continue to function
- TeamCity is no longer required for normal operation
